.docname {Try it in your browser}
.include {docs}

You can play with gol-bevy without installing anything: a web version runs right here, built from the same code as the desktop app.

.text {**Open gol-bevy in your browser →**} url:{../demo/}

It opens full screen in a new page. Press .keybinding {/} once it has loaded to see every shortcut.

## What you need

- A desktop or laptop browser with WebGL 2: any recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox, or Safari.
- A keyboard and mouse. The app is not designed for touch screens, and it needs a window at least 800 × 420 pixels.
- About 8 MB to download the first time. Your browser caches it after that.

## How it differs from the desktop app

The web version has the full simulation, drawing tools, stamps, rules, undo, and selection. Some things need the desktop app:

- **No files.** You can't open or save workspaces or open \.rle files. To bring in a pattern, paste its RLE into **Import**; to keep one, **Export** copies the board as RLE.
- **Settings are not saved.** Every visit starts with the defaults.
- **One thread.** Browsers run the simulation on a single core, so large boards and maximum speed are slower than on the desktop.
- **No screen reader support.** The web version does not expose its interface to assistive tools. Keyboard navigation and the colour themes work as on the desktop. See [Accessibility](accessibility.qd).
- **No Quit.** Close the tab instead.
- **Browser shortcuts.** A few shortcuts the browser keeps for itself, such as closing the tab, can't be used by the app.

For everything else, [install the desktop app](install.qd).

## Licences

The web version is under the same [MIT licence](https://github.com/andreashdez/gol_bevy/blob/main/LICENSE) as the desktop app. Its font is under the [SIL Open Font License](../demo/OFL.txt), and the Rust crates compiled into it are under the licences listed in [THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html](../demo/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html).
